// =========================================================================
// niri outputs - where the external display sits
// =========================================================================
//
// Include this from ~/.config/niri/config.kdl:
//
//     include "~/Work/fd44_hyprdot/niri/outputs.kdl"
//
// THE EXTERNAL GOES ON THE LEFT, which is what hypr/monitors.lua does with a
// catch-all rule - `output = ""` with `position = "auto-left"`, so ANY external
// display lands left of the laptop without naming it. niri has no auto-left:
// positions are explicit numbers, and by default it places outputs left to right
// sorted by name, which put this one on the right.
//
// ONLY THE EXTERNAL IS POSITIONED, AND THAT IS THE TRICK. niri's placement
// algorithm puts every output with an explicit position first, then puts the rest
// to the RIGHT of everything already placed. So positioning the external at
// x=-2560 and leaving eDP-1 alone lands the laptop at x=0, to its right, without
// saying so.
//
// WHICH ALSO KEEPS THE LAPTOP AT THE ORIGIN WHETHER OR NOT THE EXTERNAL IS THERE.
// hypr/monitors.lua warns about exactly this: with the laptop moved off 0,0,
// anything given coordinates by hand - `grim -g "0,0 300x40"` - captures nothing,
// because nothing is there. Undocked, eDP-1 is the only output and still lands at
// x=0.
//
// -2560 IS THE EXTERNAL'S LOGICAL WIDTH at scale 1 (2560x1440), so its right edge
// meets the laptop's left edge at 0 with no gap and no overlap. An overlap would
// not be an error: niri warns and places the output to the right instead, which is
// the behaviour this file exists to avoid.
//
// MATCHED BY MAKE, MODEL AND SERIAL rather than by DP-2, the same way
// niri/workspaces.kdl pins the workspaces - the connector moves between docks and
// is already known to be wrong once: this laptop calls it DP-2 while the notes in
// hypr/monitors.lua say DP-1.
//
// NOT SET HERE: scale. niri picked 1.5 for the laptop panel on its own, where
// hypr/monitors.lua asks for 1. That is a visible difference and changing it would
// resize the whole desktop, so it is left as niri found it and written down
// instead. The 1px overlaps in quickshell that hypr/monitors.lua calls
// "unnecessary at scale 1" are doing real work at 1.5.
// AND y=-219, WHICH CENTRES THE LAPTOP AGAINST IT. Both were top-aligned at
// y=0, and the external is 438 logical pixels taller (1440 against 1002) - so
// the bottom 438 of it had no output to its right at all. The cursor crosses
// only where two outputs are DIRECTLY ADJACENT, which niri's own config says
// and sway documents too, so down there the pointer hit a dead edge and stuck
// near the corner.
//
// The heights cannot be made to match, so the only choice is where the dead
// strip goes. Centred splits it: 219 above and 219 below, instead of 438 all
// at the bottom where a hand reaching across from the big screen actually is.
//
// THE EXTERNAL MOVES, NOT THE LAPTOP, and that is the whole reason this is
// -219 on this line rather than y=219 on an eDP-1 block. Positioning eDP-1
// would take the laptop off 0,0 - including when it is the only output - and
// the note above about `grim -g "0,0 300x40"` capturing nothing is exactly
// what would happen. Moving the external to -219 centres the pair and leaves
// the laptop at the origin: its 0..1002 sits inside the external's -219..1221,
// both centred on y=501.
output "LG Electronics LG ULTRAGEAR 106NTNH2R807" {
    position x=-2560 y=-219
}
